      "text" : "Lead D3 and deliberately duck the first diamond in dummy, playing D5. This preserves both honors and simplifies the entry problem. We have six outside winners and need three diamonds, or two diamonds plus an extra club trick. After regaining the lead, play South's D4 toward DK, then continue DQ when appropriate. Against 3-3, this establishes at least three diamond tricks with CA available to reach the remaining winners: the initial concession avoids exhausting both honors before clearing the suit. Against 4-2, count the outstanding ranks and required entries explicitly. If the ace wins the first round, the remaining honors can clear two rounds; concede the fourth diamond and use CA for the fifth. If the ace wins the second round, dummy needs an entry to cash its remaining honor and concede the fourth round, followed by another entry for the fifth. If the ace wins the third round, dummy may likewise need an entry to concede the fourth and another to cash the fifth. Where two entries are needed, cash CK and lead a club toward CT for the development entry, preserving CA for the established diamond. A successful finesse also supplies the extra club trick, so two diamond winners suffice. If the finesse loses, the extra trick and entry are unavailable; reassess defensive returns rather than assuming the fifth diamond remains reachable. If both diamond honors win, only one further diamond winner is needed, so preserve CA for it. Keep major-suit controls intact while developing diamonds, and adjust to actual returns and revealed breaks.",
